          <p>
            Quest is the striking case. Its cookies and cream powder delivers 20 g of protein in a 32 g scoop, while its salted caramel delivers 26 g in a 34 g scoop, per Quest's product pages. The cookies and cream scoop carries erythritol, corn fiber and coconut oil to build the flavor, and that space comes out of the protein. If you chose Quest because of the higher number on another flavor, you would be getting about a quarter less protein per scoop from cookies and cream.
          </p>

          <p>
            For Gold Standard the protein holds at 24 g, but sugar doubles from about 1 g in some flavors to 2 g because of the cookie crumbs. That is trivial for most people, but it adds up if you drink two or three scoops a day on a strict carbohydrate budget. ISO100 shows the opposite pattern: the cookies and cream flavor matches the brand's other flavors almost exactly, because it uses cocoa and flavoring instead of crumbs.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical lesson is simple. Never take protein or sugar numbers from a brand's headline marketing or from a different flavor's listing. Pull up the panel for cookies and cream itself, and check the serving size in grams, because a heavier scoop can hide a lower protein percentage. Our guide to the <a href="/best-low-carb-protein-powder/">best low-carb protein powder</a> goes further on reading carbohydrate and sugar alcohol lines.
